1
00:00:00,361 --> 00:00:02,364
Last video ended on this curve,

2
00:00:02,925 --> 00:00:05,670
y equals sec x, which is one over

3
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:06,392
cos x.

4
00:00:07,174 --> 00:00:09,225
At x equals zero it sits at one,

5
00:00:09,488 --> 00:00:10,110
at the bottom of a U.

6
00:00:11,259 --> 00:00:13,072
Here's a tempting guess for its derivative.

7
00:00:13,758 --> 00:00:16,846
Cos x has derivative minus sine x, so

8
00:00:16,948 --> 00:00:19,455
maybe one over cos x has derivative one

9
00:00:19,616 --> 00:00:20,638
over minus sine x.

10
00:00:21,262 --> 00:00:21,927
Just flip it.

11
00:00:22,754 --> 00:00:25,406
At x equals zero, that's one over zero,

12
00:00:25,467 --> 00:00:26,692
which doesn't even exist.

13
00:00:27,336 --> 00:00:29,374
But the tangent there is plainly flat.

14
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:31,733
So flipping the derivative fails.

15
00:00:32,559 --> 00:00:35,553
How do sec, cosec and cot really change

16
00:00:35,595 --> 00:00:36,498
when x changes?

17
00:00:37,241 --> 00:00:39,547
Let's watch sec x next to the curve

18
00:00:39,587 --> 00:00:41,232
it comes from, cos x.

19
00:00:42,014 --> 00:00:44,442
At zero, cos x is flat at its

20
00:00:44,502 --> 00:00:47,030
peak, so one over cos x is flat

21
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:48,454
at the bottom of its U.

22
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:51,575
Now move x towards pi over two.

23
00:00:52,118 --> 00:00:54,844
Cos x falls towards zero, so one over

24
00:00:54,885 --> 00:00:55,687
it rises.

25
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:59,356
And it rises faster and faster, because one

26
00:00:59,417 --> 00:01:01,119
over a tiny number is huge.

27
00:01:01,962 --> 00:01:04,265
At pi over four, cos x has slope

28
00:01:04,326 --> 00:01:07,170
about minus point seven one, and sec x

29
00:01:07,231 --> 00:01:09,634
has slope about plus one point four one.

30
00:01:10,316 --> 00:01:12,747
Nearer the wall, cos x is small, and

31
00:01:12,829 --> 00:01:13,913
sec x is steep.

32
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:17,547
So the reciprocal moves the opposite way, and

33
00:01:17,708 --> 00:01:20,638
as cos x shrinks, sec x gets steeper.

34
00:01:21,229 --> 00:01:23,723
The quotient rule video already gave us this.

35
00:01:24,508 --> 00:01:27,515
One over g has derivative minus g prime,

36
00:01:28,077 --> 00:01:28,898
over g squared.

37
00:01:29,661 --> 00:01:31,127
The minus is the opposite way.

38
00:01:31,871 --> 00:01:33,875
The g squared on the bottom is why

39
00:01:33,896 --> 00:01:36,021
a small g makes a steep curve.

40
00:01:36,843 --> 00:01:39,608
For sec x, g is cos x, and

41
00:01:39,669 --> 00:01:41,853
g prime is minus sine x.

42
00:01:42,716 --> 00:01:45,144
So the derivative is sine x over cos

43
00:01:45,186 --> 00:01:47,755
squared x, wherever cos x isn't zero.

44
00:01:48,619 --> 00:01:51,204
Split that into one over cos x, times

45
00:01:51,405 --> 00:01:52,667
sine x over cos x.

46
00:01:53,512 --> 00:01:54,946
That's sec x tan x.

47
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:57,903
At zero, tan is zero, so the slope

48
00:01:58,004 --> 00:01:59,611
is zero, the flat bottom we saw.

49
00:02:00,494 --> 00:02:02,776
At pi over four, sec x is root

50
00:02:02,817 --> 00:02:04,478
two and tan x is one,

51
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,523
so the slope is root two, about one

52
00:02:07,584 --> 00:02:09,806
point four one, just as the graph showed.

53
00:02:10,455 --> 00:02:13,139
Cosec x is one over sine x, so

54
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:15,683
the same rule works with g equals sine

55
00:02:15,704 --> 00:02:15,824
x.

56
00:02:16,626 --> 00:02:18,213
Now g prime is cos x.

57
00:02:18,857 --> 00:02:21,522
So the derivative is minus cos x, over

58
00:02:21,603 --> 00:02:24,388
sine squared x, wherever sine x isn't zero.

59
00:02:25,291 --> 00:02:26,194
Split it the same way:

60
00:02:26,696 --> 00:02:29,466
minus one over sine x, times cos x

61
00:02:29,628 --> 00:02:30,229
over sine x.

62
00:02:30,873 --> 00:02:33,127
That's minus cosec x cot x.

63
00:02:34,094 --> 00:02:36,047
Look at the graph between zero and pi

64
00:02:36,088 --> 00:02:36,470
over two.

65
00:02:37,175 --> 00:02:40,408
Sine x rises there, so cosec x falls,

66
00:02:40,871 --> 00:02:42,054
and that's its minus sign.

67
00:02:42,759 --> 00:02:45,072
At pi over four, the slope is minus

68
00:02:45,113 --> 00:02:45,574
root two.

69
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:49,687
Past pi over two, sine falls, so cosec

70
00:02:49,908 --> 00:02:50,529
rises again.

71
00:02:51,392 --> 00:02:53,758
In between, at pi over two, both are

72
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:56,186
flat, and cot of pi over two is

73
00:02:56,267 --> 00:02:58,212
zero, so the formula agrees.

74
00:02:58,634 --> 00:03:01,059
Cot x is cos x over sine x,

75
00:03:01,421 --> 00:03:02,944
which is one over tan x.

76
00:03:03,627 --> 00:03:05,492
It's a quotient, so use the quotient rule,

77
00:03:05,754 --> 00:03:06,616
just as we did for tan.

78
00:03:07,599 --> 00:03:10,344
The top's derivative is minus sine x, and

79
00:03:10,385 --> 00:03:11,567
the bottom's is cos x.

80
00:03:12,269 --> 00:03:14,315
So the top of the answer is minus

81
00:03:14,396 --> 00:03:17,505
sine x times sine x, minus cos x

82
00:03:17,686 --> 00:03:18,488
times cos x.

83
00:03:19,291 --> 00:03:21,415
Take out the minus, and what's left is

84
00:03:21,476 --> 00:03:24,101
sine squared plus cos squared, which is one.

85
00:03:24,984 --> 00:03:27,426
So the derivative of cot x is minus

86
00:03:27,588 --> 00:03:30,210
one over sine squared x, which is minus

87
00:03:30,311 --> 00:03:33,274
cosec squared x, again wherever sine x isn't

88
00:03:33,315 --> 00:03:33,635
zero.

89
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:36,896
That's tan's proof with the opposite sign.

90
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:39,326
Tan's slope is never below one.

91
00:03:39,849 --> 00:03:41,853
Cot's is never above minus one,

92
00:03:42,356 --> 00:03:44,239
so cot decreases on each branch.

93
00:03:45,122 --> 00:03:47,925
At pi over four, cosec x is root

94
00:03:47,987 --> 00:03:50,550
two, so the slope is minus root two

95
00:03:50,651 --> 00:03:52,252
squared, which is minus two.

96
00:03:53,029 --> 00:03:53,893
Notice the domains.

97
00:03:54,639 --> 00:03:57,794
Sec x, like tan x, breaks where cos

98
00:03:57,855 --> 00:03:58,678
x is zero.

99
00:03:59,222 --> 00:04:01,971
Cosec x and cot x break where sine

100
00:04:02,032 --> 00:04:04,400
x is zero, at every whole number of

101
00:04:04,441 --> 00:04:04,781
pis.

102
00:04:05,625 --> 00:04:07,946
On the graphs, sec x shares its walls

103
00:04:08,067 --> 00:04:10,648
with tan x, and cosec x shares its

104
00:04:10,689 --> 00:04:11,788
walls with cot x,

105
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:14,610
at the places where sine x crosses the

106
00:04:14,691 --> 00:04:14,971
axis.

107
00:04:15,872 --> 00:04:18,577
Each derivative lives exactly where its function does,

108
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:21,043
so write the condition with the answer,

109
00:04:21,585 --> 00:04:22,125
every time.

110
00:04:22,695 --> 00:04:23,902
Now a worked example.

111
00:04:24,445 --> 00:04:27,188
Find where the curve y equals sec x

112
00:04:27,530 --> 00:04:30,553
has a horizontal tangent, for x between minus

113
00:04:30,594 --> 00:04:33,117
pi over two and three pi over two.

114
00:04:34,024 --> 00:04:34,652
Domain first.

115
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:37,894
Cos x can't be zero, so x isn't

116
00:04:37,955 --> 00:04:38,658
pi over two.

117
00:04:39,503 --> 00:04:42,211
A horizontal tangent means the derivative is zero,

118
00:04:42,634 --> 00:04:45,282
so sec x tan x equals zero.

119
00:04:46,086 --> 00:04:48,110
Sec x is one over cos x, and

120
00:04:48,412 --> 00:04:50,195
one over a number is never zero.

121
00:04:50,678 --> 00:04:52,412
So tan x must be zero.

122
00:04:53,079 --> 00:04:55,611
In this interval, that's x equals zero and

123
00:04:55,773 --> 00:04:56,677
x equals pi.

124
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:00,845
The points are zero, one, and pi, minus

125
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:03,009
one: the bottom of one U, and the

126
00:05:03,070 --> 00:05:04,912
top of the next, upside-down U.

127
00:05:05,756 --> 00:05:07,564
Here's a pattern that halves what you memorise,

128
00:05:08,188 --> 00:05:09,614
now you know where the results come from.

129
00:05:10,337 --> 00:05:13,062
Pair each function with its co-partner: sine and

130
00:05:13,103 --> 00:05:16,068
cos, tan and cot, sec and cosec.

131
00:05:16,711 --> 00:05:18,541
Swap every name in a derivative for its

132
00:05:18,582 --> 00:05:20,150
partner, and add a minus.

133
00:05:20,874 --> 00:05:23,441
Sine to cos becomes cos to minus sine.

134
00:05:24,065 --> 00:05:26,798
Tan to sec squared becomes cot to minus

135
00:05:26,839 --> 00:05:27,542
cosec squared.

136
00:05:28,186 --> 00:05:30,853
Sec to sec tan becomes cosec to minus

137
00:05:30,894 --> 00:05:31,575
cosec cot.

138
00:05:32,238 --> 00:05:34,942
It works because each co-function is its partner

139
00:05:35,103 --> 00:05:36,806
of pi over two minus x,

140
00:05:37,327 --> 00:05:39,791
and that inside has derivative minus one.

141
00:05:40,394 --> 00:05:42,230
That's why the co-functions carry the minus.

142
00:05:44,870 --> 00:05:45,334
Your turn.

143
00:05:46,079 --> 00:05:48,887
Find the exact gradient of the curve y

144
00:05:49,089 --> 00:05:51,956
equals cosec x, at the point where x

145
00:05:52,118 --> 00:05:53,300
equals pi over six.

146
00:05:54,108 --> 00:05:54,940
Pause here to answer.

147
00:06:05,642 --> 00:06:08,114
d y by d x is minus cosec

148
00:06:08,355 --> 00:06:09,179
x cot x.

149
00:06:10,003 --> 00:06:11,888
At pi over six, sine is a half,

150
00:06:12,249 --> 00:06:14,635
so cosec x is two, and cot x

151
00:06:14,775 --> 00:06:15,437
is root three.

152
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:18,439
So the gradient is minus two root three.

153
00:06:19,123 --> 00:06:21,673
Between zero and pi over two cosec x

154
00:06:21,774 --> 00:06:24,023
is falling, so a negative slope makes sense.

155
00:06:24,686 --> 00:06:26,430
Two over root three comes from flipping the

156
00:06:26,472 --> 00:06:28,838
derivative of sine, which is the hook's mistake.

157
00:06:30,790 --> 00:06:31,194
Last one.

158
00:06:31,839 --> 00:06:36,206
Differentiate y equals cosec x plus cot x,

159
00:06:36,788 --> 00:06:38,810
and state the values of x for which

160
00:06:38,871 --> 00:06:40,173
the derivative exists.

161
00:06:40,778 --> 00:06:41,603
Pause here to answer.

162
00:06:52,415 --> 00:06:55,200
Both terms break where sine x is zero,

163
00:06:55,542 --> 00:06:57,245
so x can't be any whole number of

164
00:06:57,346 --> 00:06:57,726
pis.

165
00:06:58,528 --> 00:07:01,394
Cosec x gives minus cosec x cot x,

166
00:07:01,836 --> 00:07:04,641
and cot x gives minus cosec squared x.

167
00:07:05,183 --> 00:07:07,367
So d y by d x is minus

168
00:07:07,428 --> 00:07:10,553
cosec x cot x, minus cosec squared x,

169
00:07:10,995 --> 00:07:12,618
for x not equal to k pi.

170
00:07:13,441 --> 00:07:15,685
A plus on the cosec squared term gives

171
00:07:15,766 --> 00:07:18,110
cot the slope sign of tan, but cot

172
00:07:18,312 --> 00:07:18,972
always falls.

173
00:07:20,668 --> 00:07:23,473
So each reciprocal derivative comes from one over

174
00:07:23,554 --> 00:07:26,039
g, or from the quotient rule, and keeps

175
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:27,162
its function's domain.

176
00:07:28,125 --> 00:07:29,887
Next, the composites we've left for later:

177
00:07:30,509 --> 00:07:32,392
three to the two x, a log with

178
00:07:32,433 --> 00:07:34,697
base three, and tan of e to the

179
00:07:34,858 --> 00:07:34,977
x.

180
